### TITLE ###
Does not escape other delimiters in content - single quote with double quote
### INPUT ###
print('"hi"')
print('\"hi\"')
### STDOUT ###
"hi"
\"hi\"

### TITLE ###
Does not escape other delimiters in content - double quote with single quote
### INPUT ###
print("'hi'")
print("\'hi\'")
### STDOUT ###
'hi'
\'hi\'

### TITLE ###
Does not escape other delimiters in content - backtick with single quote
### INPUT ###
print('`hi`')
print('\`hi\`')
### STDOUT ###
`hi`
\`hi\`

### TITLE ###
Braces - a brace that cannot open an interpolation is literal
### DESCRIPTION ###
An interpolation must hold an expression. When the string ends right
after the brace, or the pair holds only whitespace, there is no room
for one - so the brace is content rather than a parse error.
### INPUT ###
print("{")
print("{}")
print("{ }")
print("prefix {")
print(`\.+*?()[]{}|^$`)
### STDOUT ###
{
{}
{ }
prefix {
\.+*?()[]{}|^$

### TITLE ###
Braces - both escapes, in every string flavor
### DESCRIPTION ###
\{ and \} are symmetric. A } with no interpolation open needs no
escape, so the last two lines are the same string written two ways.
### INPUT ###
print("\{a\}")
print('\{a\}')
print(`\{a\}`)
print("\{a}")
print(r"\{a\}")
### STDOUT ###
{a}
{a}
{a}
{a}
\{a\}

### TITLE ###
Braces - interpolation still wins wherever one is possible
### DESCRIPTION ###
Rule 2 is narrow on purpose: anything that could be an interpolation
stays one, including an expression that opens with a nested string.
### INPUT ###
name = "ana"
print("{name}")
print("{ "a" + "b" }")
print("{name:<5}|")
### STDOUT ###
ana
ab
ana  |

